"TPRG-05 A FIRST-IN-HUMAN STUDY OF A CNS-PENETRANT, SELECTIVE ERBB2 INHIBITOR, CGT4255, IN PATIENTS WHO HAVE ADVANCED SOLID TUMORS WITH ERRB2 GENETIC ALTERATIONS OR HER2 OVEREXPRESSION - PMC (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC13448434/)\nciteturn5search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: 2 days ago; This open-label, dose escalation and expansion Phase 1/1b study will evaluate safety, pharmacology, and antitumor activity of CGT4255 in adults with unresectable, locally advanced, or metastatic solid tumors harboring ERBB2-activating alterations or HER2 overexpression. ... In Parts B and C, antitumor activity of CGT4255 will be evaluated specifically in ERBB2-mutated NSCLC with brain metastases and in ERBB2-mutated or HER2-positive breast cancer with brain metastases with or without leptomeningeal disease, respectively, assessed by ORR.\nA CNS-penetrant, selective ERBB2 inhibitor targeting wild-type and mutant ERBB2 may benefit patients who have ERBB2-mutated NSCLC or HER2-positive breast cancer with brain metastases. CGT4255 is an investigational, oral, ERBB2 inhibitor, with potency against clinically relevant gene alterations and wild-type ERBB2 that spares wild-type EGFR, which may avoid EGFR-mediated toxicities including rash, mucositis, and paronychia. With evidence of high CNS penetration and activity against ERBB2-activating alterations, nonclinical data support initiation of a first-in-human study of CGT4255 (NCT07361562). This open-label, dose escalation and expansion Phase 1/1b study will evaluate safety, pharmacology, and antitumor activity of CGT4255 in adults with unresectable, locally advanced, or metastatic solid tumors harboring ERBB2-activating alterations or HER2 overexpression. The study will consist of 3 parts. In Part A dose escalation, CGT4255 will be administered in 21-day cycles to patients with ERBB2-altered advanced solid tumors (N=∼50). The primary objective is to determine the maximum tolerated or evaluated doses of CGT4255 to be carried forward to Parts B and C, through assessment of adverse events. Secondary objectives include evaluation of pharmacokinetics and antitumor activity (assessed by objective response rate [ORR] and disease control rate). In Parts B and C, antitumor activity of CGT4255 will be evaluated specifically in ERBB2-mutated NSCLC with brain metastases and in ERBB2-mutated or HER2-positive breast cancer with brain metastases with or without leptomeningeal disease, respectively, assessed by ORR. Secondary objectives include characterization of overall treatment effect, safety/tolerability, activity against CNS metastases, and pharmacokinetics in each population. This study of CGT4255 is enrolling at select sites in the United States.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nA Study of a Selective ERBB2 Inhibitor CGT4255 in Patients With Advanced Solid Tumors With ERBB2 Genetic Alterations or HER2 Overexpression | NYU Langone Health (https://clinicaltrials.med.nyu.edu/cancer/clinicaltrial/2954/study-selective-erbb2-inhibitor/?qd=75110121)\nciteturn5search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; The goal of Part B is to find the best dose of CGT4255 that works for a specific type of cancer—non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC)—that has spread to the brain (BM). ... The goal of Part C is to see if CGT4255 works in patients with ERBB2 mutations or HER2-positive breast cancer that has spread to the brain (BM), with or without leptomeningeal disease (LMD).\n\n# Perlmutter Cancer Center\n\n## Clinical Trials & Research Studies\n\n## A Study of a Selective ERBB2 Inhibitor CGT4255 in Patients With Advanced Solid Tumors With ERBB2 Genetic Alterations or HER2 Overexpression\n\n## Brief description of study\n\nThis is a study testing a new medicine called CGT4255 for patients with advanced cancers (like lung cancer or breast cancer) that have certain mutations in ERBB2 or have HER2 overexpression. CGT4255 is a new drug that works by blocking a specific protein called ERBB2, which plays a role in the growth of cancer cells. The drug works by binding to the ERBB2 protein in a way that prevents it from doing its job, which can slow down or stop cancer growth. This study is designed to test how safe and effective CGT4255 is and also to figure out the right amount of the drug that should be given to patients.\n\nIn this study, there are three parts: Part A, Part B and Part C. Part A(dose escalation), will include patients with cancers that are unresectable (can’t be surgically removed), locally advanced, or metastatic (spread to other parts of the body), with ERBB2 gene changes (like amplifications, mutations, insertions, or rearrangements) or HER2 overexpression. In this part, the drug will be given in increasing doses, starting with a small dose. The goal is to find the highest dose that patients can tolerate without serious side effects. Part B (signal seeking and dose optimization) will include patients with ERBB2 mutations (point mutations, insertions, short variants) and brain metastases (BM). The goal of Part B is to find the best dose of CGT4255 that works for a specific type of cancer—non-small cell lung cancer (NSCLC)—that has spread to the brain (BM).This part will focus on fine-tuning the dose to find the right balance between effectiveness and safety for this particular group of patients. Part C (signal seeking) will focus on testing the drug’s effectiveness in a specific cancer group that has brain involvement, especially considering the challenges of treating cancer in the brain. The goal of Part C is to see if CGT4255 works in patients with ERBB2 mutations or HER2-positive breast cancer that has spread to the brain (BM), with or without leptomeningeal disease (LMD). All patients will have frequent medical check ups and blood samples taken to see how their bodies are handling the study medicine. The study team will have special scans done for all patients to see how the cancer is responding to the study medicines. The study team will be monitoring the patients for potential side effects and safety concerns.\n\nClinical Study Identifier: s25-01032\n\nClinicalTrials.gov Identifier: NCT07361562\n\nPrincipal Investigator: Joshua K Sabari.\n\nOther Investigators: Mariam M Cheaib, Jessica Wang, Elaine Shum, Elise Roughan, Salman Rafi Punekar, David Mendoza, Natalia Cherkassky, Rajwanth Veluswamy, Melissa L Martinez, Kirby Rodriguez.\n\n### Recruitment Status\n\nOpen--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCGT4255 in Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer and ERBB2 Gene Amplification and HER2 - Clinical Trials Registry - ICH GCP (https://ichgcp.net/clinical-trials-registry/NCT07361562)\nciteturn5search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 5 days ago; # A Study of a Selective ERBB2 Inhibitor (CGT4255), in Patients With Advanced Solid Tumors ... This is an open-label, phase 1/1b study evaluating the safety, tolerability, pharmacokinetic (what the body does to the drug), pharmacodynamic (what the drug does to the body), and antitumor activity of CGT4255 in adult participants with advanced solid tumors with ERBB2 alterations or HER2 overexpression. ...   * Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer ...   * Email: trialinfo@cogentbio.com\n\n# A Study of a Selective ERBB2 Inhibitor (CGT4255), in Patients With Advanced Solid Tumors\n\nSeptember 3, 2026 updated by: Cogent Biosciences, Inc.\n\n## A Study of a Selective ERBB2 Inhibitor, CGT4255, in Patients With Advanced Solid Tumors With ERBB2 Genetic Alterations or HER2 Overexpression\n\nThis is an open-label, phase 1/1b study evaluating the safety, tolerability, pharmacokinetic (what the body does to the drug), pharmacodynamic (what the drug does to the body), and antitumor activity of CGT4255 in adult participants with advanced solid tumors with ERBB2 alterations or HER2 overexpression.\n\n## Study Overview\n\n#### Status\n\nRecruiting\n\n#### Conditions\n\n  * Non-Small Cell Lung Cancer\n  * ERBB2 Gene Amp